{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "number": 4,
  "title": "Prop 4 \u2014 Public campaign financing: what would change?",
  "fps": 30,
  "frames": 6071,
  "checked": "2026-10-19",
  "narration": "asset://17460319b76ece45ad4fc8d7e92c46c70025e61ff73097bc3b34044efb2acd6c.wav",
  "prompter_words": "words-human.json",
  "script": [
    {
      "name": "The big picture",
      "narration": "Should public money help pay for election campaigns? That is the question behind California Proposition four. Campaign financing pays for things like mailers, advertising, and campaign staff. Public financing means government money helps cover those expenses. Today, California generally bans that for state and local candidates. Some charter cities, including Los Angeles and San Francisco, already have exceptions. Proposition four would let more governments offer public financing. It does not automatically launch a program or send money to candidates."
    },
    {
      "name": "An illustrative example",
      "narration": "Imagine a first-time candidate planning how to reach voters. She has local supporters, but printing leaflets and running advertisements cost money. A future city program might match small donations. Here is a made-up example: twenty dollars from a resident, plus forty dollars from the city, gives the campaign sixty dollars. Those numbers are illustrative. Proposition four does not choose a matching rate or promise this candidate any money. The aim is to help candidates reach more voters."
    },
    {
      "name": "How it would work",
      "narration": "You might ask: who qualifies, and who decides? A future program would set detailed eligibility rules and its budget. Candidates would have to demonstrate broad support in their district and accept spending limits. Public money could not pay their fines, legal defense, or personal campaign loans. Funds reserved for education, transportation, or public safety could not be used. But other public funds spent on campaigns still have an opportunity cost. The state estimates a few hundred thousand dollars a year for guidance. That is separate from future campaign programs, whose costs depend on later decisions and could be significant."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote yes",
      "narration": "A voter favoring reform might say: I want candidates with community support to compete, even without wealthy backers. Supporters hope matching donations would make ordinary residents more important to fundraising. Voting yes gives governments that option, subject to rules. It does not guarantee less donor influence."
    },
    {
      "name": "Why someone might vote no",
      "narration": "Another voter might ask: should taxpayers pay for political campaigns? They may dislike funding candidates they oppose, or prefer other uses for public money. The measure leaves future program budgets and many details undecided. Public financing also does not eliminate private political spending. A no vote keeps the general ban and existing exceptions."
    },
    {
      "name": "What polling indicates",
      "narration": "What do polls show? In early September, PPIC found forty-two percent yes, fifty-three percent no, and five percent unsure among likely voters. A later September Berkeley poll found thirty-five percent yes, thirty-five percent no, and thirty percent undecided. These are different surveys, not a clean trend or an election forecast."
    },
    {
      "name": "Make your own decision",
      "narration": "The choice is whether to allow future public campaign financing programs, with potential benefits and public costs. Visit our website for the fuller explanation, sources, and official voter guide. Then make your own informed choice."
    }
  ],
